<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donegal Friary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1474">1474</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Donegal Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Donegal</a> Friary was a Franciscan house founded in 1474 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Hugh Roe O'Donnell</a>, lord of Tír Chonaill, and his wife Nuala at Donegal town. It was the family's chief religious foundation and burial place. Friars of Donegal later compiled the Annals of the Four Masters in the 1630s, after the friary itself had been ruined in the wars of the 1590s and 1601.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donegal-friary.jpg" alt="Donegal Friary" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas F. Borchert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donegal_Friary_Cloister_East_Range_2009_09_23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The friary stands at the mouth of the River Eske where it enters Donegal Bay, west of the town centre. A 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> account by one of its friars, translated by Charles Patrick Meehan, tells that Nuala made a journey of about 100 miles with a group of women to Ross Errilly Friary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> were holding a provincial chapter, to ask for a house in Tyrconnell. Refused at first, she warned them of God's wrath for the souls that might be lost, and friars went back with her to build beside the wharf at Donegal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nuala died later in 1474, before the building was finished, and Hugh Roe O'Donnell's second wife, Fingalla O'Brien, saw the monastery, church and cloisters completed; she later lived in austerity in a small house near the friary. In 1508 Creevelea Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-leitrim">County Leitrim</a> was founded as its daughter house. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a> the friary was used for a meeting between the rebel leaders and envoys of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, and Hugh McManus O'Donnell, who had lived many years among the friars, was buried there in 1600.</p><p>In the absence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Red Hugh O'Donnell</a>, his cousin Niall Garve O'Donnell came to terms with the English government and made the friary his base. Red Hugh attacked in 1601 but could not retake it. On 10 August 1601 a fire reached a store of Niall Garve's gunpowder, and the explosion destroyed most of the building and killed hundreds of his soldiers, among them his brother Conn Oge O'Donnell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The friary was never rebuilt. Its friars set up a new base at Bundrowes, near the River Drowes, where Mícheál Ó Cléirigh, Cú Choigríche Ó Duibhgeannáin and Fearfeasa Ó Maol Chonaire worked between 1632 and 1636. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> chalice made for the Friars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Donegal</a> in 1633 by O'Donnell's silversmith John O'Mullarkey was later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. In 1992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> beatified Concobhar Ó Duibheannaigh, a Franciscan of Donegal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Down and Conor, hanged, drawn and quartered outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1612.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donegal Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Franciscan <b>Donegal Friary</b>, founded in 1474 by Hugh Roe O'Donnell and his wife Nuala, was a key patron foundation.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-chonaill">Kingdom of Tir Chonaill</a></li></ul></section>
